Dear All Things Additive Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.3.1 connects three desktop polymer-printing tasks that are often
handled separately: adapting imported geometry, assigning the physical
material configuration and handing structured data to an external slicer.

The workflow now begins one step earlier than before. A recognised feature in
an imported STL or 3MF — a hole, a peg, a countersink, a dome — can be moved,
rotated, resized, duplicated and removed, with its original axis preserved and
its identity retained through the edit. Standards-based inserts, screw seats or
bearing seats can then use clearance values measured from a printed calibration
artefact.

Supporting that, the release presents the measured values of the selected
feature as editable fields with a live preview, applies one edit to all
features of the same kind as a single transaction, and limits recognition to
what a printer can actually produce — on a sample downloaded part, 296 of 1130
reported features were below that bound and are gone.

Each real filament spool continues to carry colour, temperatures, cooling,
retraction and material properties into the correct 3MF material slot; the
machine profiles offered are now filtered to the configured printer.

Solidon is local desktop preparation software rather than a slicer or
enterprise data platform. It runs on Windows, macOS and Linux without an
account, telemetry or cloud project store.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/

